// D1 round 7 / R7b — A DESTRUCTURING `let` OVER A DEFERRED RHS.
//
// `SemaChecker::lower_let_pat` (src/compiler/sema_stmt.cpp) used to answer an
// Error-typed RHS with a HARD error — "let <struct-pat> = expr: rhs must be a
// struct, got '<error>'" plus one "undefined variable" per bound name — and
// that error aborts the unit BEFORE the post-drain re-sema round that types an
// ADR 0021 factory-backed chain for real. So a destructuring `let` was the
// last door in the language that a container family's deferred types could not
// go through, and the SAME rhs bound by an ordinary `let` and read
// field-by-field compiled. R7b answers an Error RHS the way round 6 taught
// build_pattern_variant_data to: not "not a struct", NOT YET KNOWN — bind the
// pattern's names at error_t(), stay silent, and let the strict round type it.
//
// THE DOOR MEASURED. `b.keys()` is a `PdtCol` returned by a metaprog-emitted
// batch whose receiver type is a content-hashed family instance; in sema round
// 0 the whole `create_ctr::<typeof(Led)>(…)` → `leaf_batches()` →
// `next_batch()` → `unwrap()` chain is Kind::Error. The deferral branch FIRES
// here — measured with a fire-count print inside the branch, which counted 1,
// then removed — and the strict round resolves the pattern against `PdtCol`.
//
// ── SUPERSEDED 2026-08-12 (D1 round 8 / S0). WHAT ROUND 7 CLAIMED HERE ────
//
// Round 7 wrote, and this paragraph kept: "the binding half of the deferred
// destructure is unpinnable today … a content hash which no source file can
// spell … Closing the gap needs a stdlib change, not a test change." That is
// FALSE, and it was false when it was written. Measured in round 8:
//   • a content-hashed family name DOES resolve in PATTERN position — the
//     diagnostic the binding half actually produced was «unknown field
//     'found'», not «unknown type», i.e. the pattern's TYPE had resolved and
//     the FIELD lookup was what failed;
//   • the field lookup failed because the pattern path called `field_type_of`
//     with TWO arguments while the expr path passes the receiver's
//     `pkg_name()` as a third (sema_stmt.cpp / sema.cpp). Without the hint the
//     lookup falls back to an import-scope-dependent search, so a
//     metaprog-EMITTED struct in another package is not found. An OVER-REFUSAL
//     in sema, one layer below the deferral — nothing to do with the stdlib.
//   • what does NOT work is the hash name in TYPE position (`let cur:
//     Hs…Cur = …` errs «unknown type»). So the pattern name is the only
//     spelling available, and it is the one used below.
// The gap is closed here rather than in a new fixture: the sentence that was
// wrong is the same sentence this file exists to make true.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THE BINDING HALF ALSO PINS. `Hs352959f3caf5b795Cur` is a function of
// the container DEFINITION above (kind + entry + measures), so this fixture now
// also pins the family MANGLER: change the hash scheme or this `container Led`
// and this test reds on the name, not on the rule. That is the stated price of
// pinning the binding half at all — there is no unhashed spelling of the type.
//
// VALUES, not just compilation: the same chain is read for real around the
// destructure (two rows, keys 1+2, vals 10+20 ⇒ sum 33), so a deferral that
// left the unit half-typed would answer wrong instead of compiling silently.
package bc_d1r7_b1_destructure_deferred;

use logos.lcm.canon.container_item;
use logos.lcm.canon.metaclass;
use logos.mem.bt.map;
use logos.mem.bt.memstore;
use logos.mem.sync;
use logos.lang.stream;
use logos.mem.bt.batch;     // PdtCol

pub container Led {
    kind ordered_map;
    entry { key: u64, val: u64 }
    measure count;
    measure max(key);
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut store: Arc<MemoryStore> = store_new();
    let snap: Arc<dyn Snapshot> = store_fork_arc(&mut store);
    let mut c = create_ctr::<typeof(Led)>(&snap, 1u64);
    c.insert(1u64, 10u64);
    c.insert(2u64, 20u64);

    let mut n: u64 = 0u64;
    let mut sum: u64 = 0u64;
    {
        let mut s = c.leaf_batches();
        let b = s.next_batch().unwrap();
        let PdtCol { .. } = b.keys();      // THE DOOR: rhs is Kind::Error in round 0
        let kc: PdtCol = b.keys();
        let vc: PdtCol = b.vals();
        n = b.len();
        let mut j: u64 = 0u64;
        while j < n { sum = sum + kc.at(j) + vc.at(j); j = j + 1u64; }
    }
    // THE BINDING HALF (D1 round 8 / S0). Same deferred chain, and now the
    // pattern BINDS a name and the program READS its value in both directions
    // — a fix that resolved the field but bound garbage would answer 3 or 4.
    {
        let Hs352959f3caf5b795Cur { found, .. } = c.seek(1u64);
        if !found { return 3i32; }     // key 1 was inserted
    }
    {
        let Hs352959f3caf5b795Cur { found, .. } = c.seek(9u64);
        if found { return 4i32; }      // key 9 was not
    }

    if n != 2u64 { return 1i32; }
    if sum != 33u64 { return 2i32; }   // (1+10) + (2+20)
    return 0i32;
}
